<div><img width="800" height="415" src="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_feat.jpg?w=800" class="attachment-large size-large wp-post-image" alt="" style="margin: 0 auto; margin-bottom: 15px;" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_feat.jpg 1280w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_feat.jpg?resize=250,130 250w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_feat.jpg?resize=400,208 400w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_feat.jpg?resize=800,415 800w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px" data-attachment-id="881336" data-permalink="https://hackaday.com/2025/11/25/the-zen-must-flow-from-arrakis-sand-table/sandtable_feat/" data-orig-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_feat.jpg" data-orig-size="1280,664" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}" data-image-title="sandtable_feat" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_feat.jpg?w=400" data-large-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_feat.jpg?w=800" /></div><p>In <em>Dune</em>, the Fremen people of Arrakis practice an odd future hybrid religion called &#8220;zensunni.&#8221; This adds an extra layer of meaning to the title of [Mark Rehorst]&#8217;s <a href="https://hackaday.io/project/204501-arrakis-30-coffee-sand-table" target="_blank">Arrakis 3.0 sand table,</a> given that the inspiration for the robotic sand table seems to be Zen gardens from Japan.</p>
<p>The dunes on the tabletop version of Arrakis owe nothing to sand worms, but are instead created a rolling metal ball. With all workings happening below, it looks quite magical to the uninitiated, but of course it&#8217;s not magic: it&#8217;s magnets. Just beneath the tabletop and its sands, the steel ball is being dragged along by the magnetic field of a powerful neodynium magnet.</p>
<p><a href="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg" target="_blank"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-attachment-id="881337" data-permalink="https://hackaday.com/2025/11/25/the-zen-must-flow-from-arrakis-sand-table/sandtable_detail/" data-orig-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg" data-orig-size="2000,1598" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}" data-image-title="sandtable_detail" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg?w=400" data-large-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg?w=782" class="alignright size-medium wp-image-881337" src="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg?w=400" alt="" width="400" height="320" srcset="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg 2000w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg?resize=250,200 250w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg?resize=400,320 400w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg?resize=782,625 782w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/sandtable_detail.jpg?resize=1536,1227 1536w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 400px) 100vw, 400px" /></a>That magnet is mounted in a CoreXY motion system that owes more than a little bit to modern 3D printers. Aside from the geometry, it&#8217;s using the standard G6 belt we see so often, along with a Duet3D mainboard, NEMA 17 steppers, and many 3D printed parts to hold its aluminum extrusions together. Thanks to that printer-inspired motion system, the ball can whirl around at 2000 mm/s, though [Mark] prefers to run slower: the demo video below shows operation at 1000 mm/s before the sand has been added.</p>
<p>This build was designed for ease of construction and movement: sized at 2&#8217;x4&#8242; (about 61 cm x 122 cm), it fits through doors and fits an off-the-shelf slab of coffee table glass, something that [Mark] wishes he&#8217;d considered when building version two. That&#8217;s the nice thing about jumping in on a project someone&#8217;s been iterating for a while: you&#8217;ve got the benefit of learning from their mistakes. You can see the roots of this design, and what has changed, from <a href="https://hackaday.com/2020/09/22/watch-a-fast-sand-plotter-plow-patterns-at-speed/">the one he showed us in 2020. </a></p>
<p>Naturally you&#8217;re not limited to CoreXY for a sand table, though it is increasingly popular &#8212; we&#8217;ve seen examples with <a href="https://hackaday.com/2025/06/30/sand-drawing-table-inspired-by-sisyphus/">polar mechanisms</a> and even <a href="https://hackaday.com/2018/10/18/sandbot-happily-and-tirelessly-rolls-patterns-in-sand/">a SCARA arm.</a></p>
